CONTROL ACCOUNTS

Control accounts provide an independent summary of a group of personal ledger balances. They strengthen internal checking and can help locate errors.

TRADE RECEIVABLES CONTROL ACCOUNT
Typical debit entries:
• opening receivables;
• credit sales;
• dishonoured items where applicable;
• interest charged where applicable.

Typical credit entries:
• cash received;
• discounts allowed;
• sales returns;
• bad debts;
• contra entries;
• closing receivables.

TRADE PAYABLES CONTROL ACCOUNT
Typical credit entries:
• opening payables;
• credit purchases;
• relevant charges.

Typical debit entries:
• cash paid;
• discounts received;
• purchase returns;
• contra entries;
• closing payables.

RECONCILIATION
If the control account does not agree with the list of individual ledger balances, investigate omissions, posting errors, incorrect totals, contra entries, returns, discounts and opening balances.

CASH BOOK
A cash book records cash and bank transactions and can function as both a book of original entry and part of the ledger.

UPDATED CASH BOOK
Items appearing in the bank statement but not yet recorded in the cash book should be entered first. Examples can include bank charges, interest, direct debits, standing orders, direct credits and dishonoured cheques where relevant.

BANK RECONCILIATION
The purpose is to explain the difference between the adjusted cash-book balance and the bank-statement balance.

Common causes:
• unpresented cheques;
• outstanding deposits;
• bank charges not recorded;
• direct debits/credits;
• errors by the bank;
• errors in the cash book.

A reliable method:
1. Update the cash book for items known from the bank statement.
2. Recalculate the adjusted cash-book balance.
3. Prepare the bank reconciliation using timing differences and legitimate bank errors.
4. Check that the final balance agrees with the bank statement after the listed reconciling items.

BANK OVERDRAFT
An overdraft is a credit balance in the bank column of the cash book and represents a liability, subject to the exact presentation required.

INTERNAL CONTROL
Internal control aims to protect assets, ensure reliable records, improve operational efficiency and reduce the opportunity for fraud and error.

Examples:
• separation of duties;
• authorisation of transactions;
• sequential documents;
• physical safeguards;
• independent checking;
• bank reconciliation;
• inventory counts;
• password/access controls;
• review of exception reports.

ACCOUNTING AND ICT
Computerised systems can improve speed, storage, reporting and consistency, but they do not eliminate risks. Access controls, backups, audit trails, user permissions and validation remain important.

EXAM APPLICATION
If a bank statement shows a bank charge of $80 that has not been entered in the cash book:
Dr Bank charges $80
Cr Bank $80
The adjusted cash-book balance then becomes the starting point for the reconciliation.

FINANCIAL REPORTING

Financial reporting communicates financial performance and position to users. The syllabus specifically includes a range of International Accounting Standards (IAS) and financial statement analysis.

IAS 1 — PRESENTATION OF FINANCIAL STATEMENTS
Focuses on the presentation and structure of financial statements and the information needed to make them understandable and comparable.

Key principles for exam purposes:
• clear classification;
• comparative information;
• appropriate aggregation and disaggregation;
• consistent presentation;
• fair and understandable communication.

IAS 2 — INVENTORIES
Inventory is measured using the syllabus basis of cost and net realisable value. Cost includes appropriate purchase and conversion/other directly attributable costs. Inventory is recognised as an expense when the related goods are sold.

IAS 7 — STATEMENT OF CASH FLOWS
Cash flows are classified into:
Operating activities — main revenue-producing activities.
Investing activities — acquisition/disposal of long-term assets and investments.
Financing activities — changes in equity and borrowings.

A cash flow statement explains why the cash balance changed. Profit is not the same as cash generated.

IAS 8 — ACCOUNTING POLICIES, CHANGES IN ACCOUNTING ESTIMATES AND ERRORS
Accounting policies are the principles and methods used in preparing statements.
A change in estimate arises when new information changes an amount previously estimated.
Errors are mistakes or omissions in previously issued information.

The accounting treatment depends on whether the item is a policy change, estimate change or prior-period error.

IAS 10 — EVENTS AFTER THE REPORTING PERIOD
Events after the reporting period are events occurring between the reporting date and the date the financial statements are authorised.

The key distinction is between:
• adjusting events — provide evidence of conditions existing at the reporting date;
• non-adjusting events — indicate conditions arising after the reporting date.

IAS 16 — PROPERTY, PLANT AND EQUIPMENT
PPE is recognised and subsequently accounted for according to applicable recognition and measurement principles. Important concepts include:
• cost;
• useful life;
• residual value;
• depreciation;
• carrying amount;
• disposal;
• impairment;
• subsequent expenditure where relevant.

IAS 36 — IMPAIRMENT OF ASSETS
An asset is impaired when its carrying amount exceeds its recoverable amount. A recoverable amount comparison can lead to recognition of an impairment loss.

IAS 38 — INTANGIBLE ASSETS
An intangible asset is an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance, such as qualifying development-related or intellectual-property assets. Recognition depends on the relevant criteria.

REPORTING QUALITY
Good financial reporting should be:
• relevant;
• faithfully represented;
• comparable;
• understandable;
• supported by appropriate evidence;
• internally consistent and clearly presented.

EXAM TIP
When a question names an IAS, do not merely write its number. State the accounting issue, the relevant rule and the effect on the financial statements.

INTERNAL CONTROL

Internal controls are policies and procedures designed to safeguard assets, maintain reliable records, improve efficiency and ensure compliance.

CONTROL OBJECTIVES
• prevent/detect errors;
• prevent/detect fraud;
• protect cash and inventory;
• ensure transactions are authorised;
• maintain reliable accounting information;
• support accountability.

SEPARATION OF DUTIES
Where possible, different people should:
• authorise;
• record;
• hold custody of assets;
• independently check.
This reduces the chance that one person can both commit and conceal an irregularity.

CASH CONTROLS
• numbered receipts;
• daily banking;
• independent cash counts;
• petty-cash imprest systems;
• bank reconciliation;
• authorisation of payments;
• limits on cash handling.

INVENTORY CONTROLS
• authorised stores requisitions;
• restricted access;
• perpetual records where appropriate;
• periodic physical counts;
• investigation of discrepancies;
• segregation of obsolete/damaged stock.

PURCHASE CONTROLS
Typical sequence:
Purchase requisition → purchase order → goods received note → supplier invoice → matching/verification → authorised payment.

SALES CONTROLS
Customer order → dispatch documentation → invoice → receivable ledger → receipt/bank entry.

COMPUTERISED ACCOUNTING
Advantages:
• speed;
• automatic calculations;
• easier reporting;
• data storage;
• integration;
• reduced repetitive work.

Risks:
• unauthorised access;
• incorrect input;
• software failure;
• cyberattack;
• data corruption;
• inadequate backups;
• over-reliance on automated calculations.

IT CONTROLS
• user IDs and strong passwords;
• role-based access;
• backups;
• audit trails;
• validation checks;
• antivirus/security controls;
• change management;
• disaster recovery.

AUDIT TRAIL
An audit trail allows a transaction to be traced from source document through accounting records to financial statements, and potentially backwards from statements to source evidence.

BUSINESS ETHICS AND INTERNAL CONTROL
A strong control environment supports transparency and ethical behaviour. However, controls can be overridden by management collusion, so independent review remains important.

EXAM EVALUATION
If asked whether a control is effective, explain:
1. the risk;
2. the control;
3. how it reduces the risk;
4. any limitation;
5. an additional control if appropriate.
